  <section class="section" id="implications" aria-labelledby="imp-title">
    <h2 id="imp-title">What this means</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>Phones cost connection even in unpublished studies.</strong> Pooling the nonsignificant studies did not erase the effect. It is small (d from −.15 to −.21 in the models), but small effects can add up. A person who is a little less connected at every meal and wait could notice the difference over a day.</p>
      <p><strong>The waiting-alone result was not what we predicted.</strong> We expected no phone cost when nobody else was there. The effect was not reliably different, and a phone did not make waiting alone feel better either. Our one nonsocial situation cannot settle this.</p>
      <p><strong>Mood depends on what the phone is for.</strong> Phones lowered affect valence when they competed with people. They did not when the phone served a current goal. The published getting-directions study found two paths that point in opposite directions: finding the building faster felt good, and feeling less connected felt bad. A null main effect is consistent with those two cancelling.</p>
      <p><strong>Why men?</strong> We did not test the reasons. The paper lists four candidates. Men may use phones more in social settings, though earlier research suggests women use phones more. Men may be more distractible, though earlier research finds no gender gap in attention. Women may follow stricter norms about phones in company. Or women may use phones in more social ways, such as messaging. Each could be tested by measuring how people use their phones during a conversation.</p>
      <p><strong>Limits.</strong> The samples were mostly college-aged people from British Columbia, and most situations were pleasant social ones. Taking a phone away may add confounds. We analyzed data we had already seen, so our registered plans could have been shaped by that. The gender result was not registered and needs a new, confirmatory test. We kept everyone as randomly assigned, which makes the effect for people who actually followed instructions a little larger than the numbers here.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="files" aria-labelledby="files-title">
    <h2 id="files-title">How this page was made</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every coefficient and test comes from the printed paper. The paper’s data, code and materials are public on <a href="https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/X73M9">OSF</a>, including the supplemental tables and the preregistrations. I have not re-run the models on the raw data.</p>
      <p>For the by-situation forest plots, I derived each 95% interval from the group size, with the standard error equal to the square root of 4 over N plus d squared over 2N. The same approach reproduces the paper’s own forest plot. The gender averages carry intervals of 1.96 standard errors from the printed standard errors. The paper’s Table 6 is partly garbled in my copy, so I used the values the text prints. The rain plots in the paper’s Figure 2 need the individual data, so I show the averages instead.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
